00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.940
STEALING $120M:
This man stole $120 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:08.700
from Google and Facebook and nearly got away with&nbsp;
it. Hearing this, you’re probably thinking that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:15.180
this guy, Evaldas Rimasauskas, was some sort of&nbsp;
insane hacker or insider. He must’ve breached&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:20.580
through Google’s security systems or used insider&nbsp;
knowledge to siphon money from Facebook. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:27.060
in reality, he was just an ordinary man from&nbsp;
Lithuania, and the scheme he used wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:33.420
especially notable either. He used a classic email&nbsp;
phishing scheme, yet somehow, he was able to trick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:39.600
some of the smartest people and companies in the&nbsp;
world. The craziest part was that these companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:45.660
didn’t even know they were being scammed, and he&nbsp;
could’ve easily gotten away with it if didn’t try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:51.900
to push his luck. You see, Evaldas didn’t just set&nbsp;
out to hit a jackpot and retire. He was looking to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:58.800
defraud as many companies for as much money as&nbsp;
possible. So, everytime he hoodwinked a company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:05.940
he would just do it again to the same company or&nbsp;
next company. Originally, he stole $23 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:12.000
from Google throughout 2013. At that point,&nbsp;
that’s literally generational wealth, but our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:18.900
man Evaldas wasn’t happy. Just 2 years later, he&nbsp;
would target Facebook and bag another $98 million.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:25.080
This greed would eventually get him noticed though&nbsp;
and these companies would notify the FBI who would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:30.180
track him down to Lithuania. They would of course&nbsp;
arrest him and he would be sentenced to 5 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:36.480
of jail. But, the same could not be said about&nbsp;
his associates who were never found. Apparently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:40.980
Evaldas would wire money the stole funds all&nbsp;
around the world as soon as he received them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:46.380
So, who knows how many people were involved&nbsp;
and how much they were each able to get away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:52.800
with. But, all of this begs the question. How&nbsp;
did he do it? How was he able to outsmart the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:58.560
smartest companies in the world and get away with&nbsp;
it for years? And what does that mean regarding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:03.660
the security of the data that all of us have&nbsp;
stored on these platforms? Well, here’s the story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:10.020
of Evaldas Rimasauskas, the guy who stole $120&nbsp;
million from tech companies and nearly got away.

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:13.312
FINDING THE WEAK SPOT:
Taking a look back, the story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.312 --> 00:02:21.540
of Evaldas Rimasauskas traces back to the early&nbsp;
2010s to Lithuania. Like any country, Lithuania&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:26.520
is mostly filled with awesome law-abiding&nbsp;
people and great culture, but unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:32.580
they are notorious for one crime: car theft. If&nbsp;
you’ve ever heard of someone’s car being stolen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:37.500
it was probably dismantled within no time&nbsp;
and shipped off to Lithuania. From there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:42.960
the car parts are listed on sites like eBay like&nbsp;
nothing happened. Their listings are usually way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:49.200
cheaper than everything else on the market for&nbsp;
well obvious reasons. But, by the early 2010s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:54.300
authorities were starting to catch on and they&nbsp;
were busting these car rings left and right. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.300 --> 00:03:00.660
our man, Evaldas, needed a new strategy, something&nbsp;
that wasn’t so hot and risky, something that no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:06.540
one was even paying attention to. One of the main&nbsp;
problems with carjacking for criminals is that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:11.340
basically always gets notified to the police.&nbsp;
I mean, who wouldn’t report to the police that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:16.380
they had their car stolen. Not to mention, car&nbsp;
jacking wasn’t even all that lucrative given how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:20.820
much risk was involved. If you wanted to make&nbsp;
a million dollars for example and you sold each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:26.820
car part for $500 on average, you would need&nbsp;
2000 car parts. This means that you would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:34.260
to steal dozens resulting dozens police reports.&nbsp;
Obviously, not a very good risk to reward ratio,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:40.320
so Evaldas decided to hatch up his own plan.&nbsp;
He first asked himself where can I get a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:46.800
of money without the victim even noticing and the&nbsp;
answer became clear pretty quickly: filthy rich&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:53.520
tech companies. There was only one problem, he&nbsp;
was by no means the first or last person to think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:58.680
of this idea. Tech companies know all about how&nbsp;
they’re vulnerable to cyber attacks not just to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:04.500
steal money but to steal personal data, top secret&nbsp;
technology, and confidential information. Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:11.280
even the CIA stores their information on Amazon&nbsp;
servers. So, maintaining a secure public image&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:16.620
is no doubt a top priority for these companies,&nbsp;
and I don’t you’d be surprised to hear that these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:21.420
tech companies invest tens of billions into&nbsp;
cybersecurity. But, this really only protects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:26.580
these companies against cyber threats. People who&nbsp;
are trying to directly hack into their systems to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:32.280
wreak havoc. Evaldas wasn’t even considering this&nbsp;
option because well he didn’t even stand a chance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:38.100
against the IT departments of these companies. So,&nbsp;
Evaldas looked to identify a weaker entry point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:44.640
somewhere money was already exiting the company.&nbsp;
This left Evaldas with really only one options:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:51.180
the payroll department. For most companies, paying&nbsp;
employees and other businesses is by far their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:57.960
biggest expense and Google and Facebook were no&nbsp;
exception. Google, for example, spent $56 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:03.840
on payroll last year. But, paying employees is&nbsp;
pretty a streamlined and high scrutinized process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:09.900
so this wasn’t exactly a great entry point.&nbsp;
Paying businesses, however, was highly custom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:16.380
and usually handled on a case by case basis. So,&nbsp;
if you posed as a business, you would only have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:21.060
trick the handful of people who are approving&nbsp;
a given invoice not the company’s entire IT&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:26.340
department or security infrastructure. And&nbsp;
with that, Evaldas had his entry point.

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:28.780
HITTING THE JACKPOT:&nbsp;

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:34.980
Companies like Google and Facebook payout&nbsp;
money to thousands if not tens of thousands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:40.020
of different companies for all sorts of reasons&nbsp;
from manufacturing and logistics to research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:44.400
and legal services. While newer partners may&nbsp;
get more scrutiny when it comes to billing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:48.240
most established partners just send over&nbsp;
invoices and these companies approve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:52.920
them without a second thought. So, Evaldas&nbsp;
decided to pose as one of these partners,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:59.580
a company called Quanta Computer. Like me, most of&nbsp;
ya’ll have probably never heard of this company so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:04.620
here’s what they do. Quanta is a Taiwanese&nbsp;
manufacturer that specializes in producing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:09.900
enterprise hardware like servers. They’re actually&nbsp;
super reputable in the industry as they work with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:16.320
basically every tech company you can think of.&nbsp;
This includes Apple Dell, HP, Amazon, Cisco, LG,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:21.840
Sony, Microsoft, Facebook, and so much more. So,&nbsp;
I don’t think you’d be surprised to hear that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:27.840
pull in over $40 billion worth of revenue and $1.5&nbsp;
billion worth of profit every year. Similarly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:32.760
Google and Facebook weren’t surprised when they&nbsp;
received tabs worth a couple million dollars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:37.920
from Quanta. Evaldas went ahead and created fake&nbsp;
invoices and contracts and signed them with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:42.720
name of various leaders at Quanta. He would then&nbsp;
send over these documents to Google and Facebook&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:48.240
from a Quanta domain email address. You know those&nbsp;
random emails that you get from Apple and Amazon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:53.160
stating that you’ve been charged X amount.&nbsp;
Well, this is exaclty what Evaldas was doing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:58.080
but instead of targeting elderly folks who don’t&nbsp;
know better for a few hundred bucks, Evaldas was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:04.620
targeting tech giants for millions. Same tactic,&nbsp;
completely different scale. With all the articles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:09.180
that these companies put out about identifying and&nbsp;
stopping phishing, you would think that the staff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:12.388
at these companies would know better, but it turns&nbsp;
out they didn’t. They would fall for this scam not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.388 --> 00:07:13.440
once or twice or three times. They would fall for&nbsp;
it over and over and over and over again. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:19.260
it’s not like Evaldas charged Google $23 million&nbsp;
and Facebook $98 million in one fell swoop.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.260 --> 00:07:23.940
According to the department of justice, Evaldas&nbsp;
would regularly send multi million dollar invoices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:29.700
to these companies. If we assume that each invoice&nbsp;
was worth $4-5 million, Evaldas trolled Google 5&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:34.500
times and Facebook a laughable 20 times. But,&nbsp;
it doesn’t even stop right there. So, alright&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:39.120
whatever, people at these companies didn’t put too&nbsp;
much scrutiny on these invoices and they approved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:44.100
them. They must have some sort of established&nbsp;
payment system with these companies right? So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:48.420
even if they accidently made an unnecessary&nbsp;
payment, it would be no problem getting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.420 --> 00:07:53.460
money back from the real company. Surely, these&nbsp;
companies weren’t sending tens of millions to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:58.320
some random ass Latvian bank accounts detailed&nbsp;
in the invo..they were sending tens of millions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:03.540
to some random ass Latvian bank accounts detailed&nbsp;
in the invoice. To be honest, I’m not gonna defend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:08.580
Evaldas, but you can’t feel too bad for these&nbsp;
companies either. There’s really no one to blame&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:14.220
but themselves and their gross negligence. In this&nbsp;
situation, any normal person would have no chance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:20.760
of any recourse, but given the power and status&nbsp;
of these companies, you can bet they got justice.

00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:22.260
IT ALL FALLS DOWN:
Evaldas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:29.580
was smart enough to not hold onto the money&nbsp;
in the same account that he received it. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.580 --> 00:08:34.380
would instantly spread the money across the world&nbsp;
into bank accounts in Latvia, Cyprus, Slovakia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:39.780
Lithuania, Hungary, and Hong Kong. Considering&nbsp;
this, it’s surprising that he didn’t take more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:47.520
precautions to secure his own freedom. With&nbsp;
all this money, he could’ve easily moved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:52.740
to a non-extraditing country like the United&nbsp;
Arab Emirates and lived it up in Dubai. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.740 --> 00:08:57.300
he would stick to Lithuania. I guess, he&nbsp;
must’ve felt that he was safe behind his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.300 --> 00:09:03.540
computer screen and that as long as the money was&nbsp;
dispersed and untrackable he was good. This was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.540 --> 00:09:07.620
blunder though. As soon as Google and Facebook&nbsp;
noticed some discrepancies in their accounting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:13.440
they would identify the fraudulent charges and&nbsp;
notify the FBI. For the FBI, tracking down someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:18.960
like Evaldas was relatively easy and they would&nbsp;
have him cornered in no time. With no plausible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:24.060
deniability, Evaldas would just plead guilty,&nbsp;
and eventually, he’d be sentenced to 5 years in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.060 --> 00:09:28.800
prison. If you’re wondering what happened to the&nbsp;
money, well, Evaldas would forfeit $49 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:33.840
of it and he was ordered to pay $27 million in&nbsp;
restitution. Facebook and Google would claim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:38.520
this as a victory as they were technically&nbsp;
able to recover the majority of the funds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.520 --> 00:09:43.740
atleast on paper. It’s not clear if Evaldas has&nbsp;
paid the restitution yet or if he ever will,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.740 --> 00:09:49.140
so that still leaves $44 to $71 million&nbsp;
worth of stolen funds unaccounted for. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.140 --> 00:09:54.240
I don’t really think Google or Facebook really&nbsp;
cares about recovering these funds. For Google,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:58.920
the total stolen funds are equivalent&nbsp;
to 3.5 hours of profit. For Facebook,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:04.380
the total stolen funds are equivalent to 37&nbsp;
hours of profit. So, for these companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:09.600
catching Evaldas and putting him behind bars was&nbsp;
more about the principle and making a statement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:15.480
than actually recovering the funds. Spending more&nbsp;
effort on recovering the remaining funds is really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:21.240
just not worth it for these companies. So, Evaldas&nbsp;
may very well have a fortune to return to when he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:27.720
eventually gets released around the end of 2024.&nbsp;
But, all of this still leaves the question, how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:32.760
was he able to pull of something so simple against&nbsp;
these companies. Well, the brightside is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:38.760
while this incident is truly quite embarassing for&nbsp;
these companies, it’s not indicative of their IT&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:44.940
or cybersecurity. As far as we know, their systems&nbsp;
are still as strong and secure as ever. And this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.940 --> 00:10:49.260
incident has little to do with anything&nbsp;
other than gross negligence and oversight.

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:54.420
